meta {
  name: 03. Connect (Query Token - Browser Style)
  type: ws
  seq: 3
}

ws {
  url: {{wsBaseUrl}}/api/v1/ws?token={{token}}
  body: ws
  auth: none
}

params:query {
  token: {{token}}
}

body:ws {
  name: ws.ping
  content: '''
    {
      "id": "ping-1",
      "event": "ws.ping"
    }
  '''
}

docs {
  # Connect WebSocket (Query Token — Browser Style)

  **Protocol:** `WebSocket` (`ws://` / `wss://`)
  **Endpoint:** `/api/v1/ws?token=<access_token>`
  **Auth:** Query Parameter `token` (Access Token)

  ## Description
  Primary browser-compatible handshake flow. Because browser WebSocket clients cannot attach custom `Authorization` headers during the upgrade handshake, pass the JWT access token via `?token=<access_token>`.

  ## Lifecycle on Connect
  1. Server validates the JWT signature, expiry, `token_type == "access"`, and Redis blacklist.
  2. Server upgrades the connection (`101 Switching Protocols`) and immediately pushes `ws.connected`:
     ```json
     {
       "event": "ws.connected",
       "data": {
         "conn_id": "d2f1c8e4-9b7a-4b2c-8e1a-1234567890ab",
         "user_id": 7
       },
       "timestamp": "2026-11-26T10:30:00Z"
     }
     ```
  3. All personal, group (`channel: "group:<id>"`), and broadcast notifications now stream automatically without any `ws.subscribe` message.
  4. Use the pre-configured `ws.ping` frame to test round-trip connectivity (`ws.pong` reply).

  ## Prerequisite
  Run `Auth -> 05. Login (Identifier + Password)` first so `{{token}}` is populated.

  ## Errors (HTTP JSON before upgrade)
  * `401 MISSING_TOKEN` — `token` query parameter missing
  * `401 TOKEN_EXPIRED` — Access token expired (refresh via `POST /api/v1/auth/refresh`)
  * `401 TOKEN_INVALID` — Invalid token or refresh token used instead of access token
  * `401 TOKEN_REVOKED` — Token has been revoked
  * `403 Forbidden` — `Origin` host not allowed by `WS_ORIGIN_PATTERNS`
}
